---
import Guide from "../../layouts/Guide.astro"
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro"
import DocLink from "../../components/DocLink.astro"
import Example from "../../components/Example/index"
---

<Guide overline="Guide">
  <Fragment slot="title">Customizing</Fragment>
  <Fragment slot="preamble">
    Theme tokens change the whole library. When you want to change one
    component, or one instance of it, reach for its <code>--_</code> custom properties.
  </Fragment>

  <section>
    <h2 id="your-theme">Your theme</h2>
    <p>
      A custom theme is a short list of <DocLink href="/guide/theme-tokens"
        >theme tokens</DocLink
      >. Put them in the <code>theme.custom</code> layer: it sits right after the
      defaults in <code>theme.base</code>, so it beats them, and every component
      and utility still beats it. Only list what you change, and new tokens in
      later releases keep their defaults.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`@layer theme.custom {
  :where(html) {
    --ui-border-radius: 4px;
    --ui-density: 0.9;
    --ui-font-body: "Inter", var(--font-sans);
    --ui-font-heading: "Fraunces", var(--font-serif);
    --ui-palette-chroma: 0.35;
    --ui-palette-hue: 265;
  }
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <p>
      Load it after the library, or after <code>layers.css</code> when you import
      single files. If it might load first, start it with the two <code
        >@layer</code
      > statements from <code>layers.css</code>, like the reference themes and
      the generated file do, so <code>theme.custom</code> still lands after the defaults.
      The <DocLink href="/guide/theme-generator">Theme generator</DocLink> writes
      one for you, and <DocLink href="/guide/theming">Theming</DocLink> explains each
      token.
    </p>
    <h3 id="reference-themes">Reference themes</h3>
    <p>
      Four reference themes ship with the package, as far apart as we could make
      them. None of them touches component CSS: each one is a list of tokens in <code
        >theme.custom</code
      >, and the tests run every stress test page with each of them, in light
      and dark mode. Try them on this site in the theme drawer in the header, or
      in the <DocLink href="/guide/theme-generator">Theme generator</DocLink>.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>brutalist</code>: thick black borders, hard offset shadows on
        cards, buttons and fields, square corners, heavy type and a hot pink
        accent on yellow.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>classic</code>: a blue primary, small corners, a soft focus glow
        and familiar proportions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>groovy</code>: pink drifting to coral across the palette, pill
        buttons, round corners, rounded and handwritten fonts, soft colored
        shadows and roomy spacing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>minimal</code>: monochrome grays with a near-black primary,
        compact controls and a faint shadow.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      code={`@import "opui-css/css/imports.css";
@import "opui-css/themes/brutalist.css";`}
      lang="css"
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="component-properties">Component properties</h2>
    <p>
      There are two kinds of custom properties. <DocLink
        href="/guide/theme-tokens">Theme tokens</DocLink
      >, like <code>--ui-primary</code> or <code>--ui-button-size</code>, are
      set on
      <code>html</code> and inherited by everything. Change one and every component
      that reads it follows.
    </p>
    <p>
      Component properties start with <code>--_</code>, like <code
        >--_accent</code
      > or <code>--_icon-size</code>. Each component declares them on itself and
      builds its styles from them. They're the knobs for that one component, and
      the API for customizing it.
    </p>
    <p>For example, Button reads these:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>--_accent</code>: the accent color. Fills, borders, text, hover
        and active states are all derived from it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--_bg-color</code> and <code>--_text-color</code>: the current
        background and text color.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--_duration</code> and <code>--_ease</code>: the transition
        duration and easing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--_icon-size</code>: the size of a direct child <code>svg</code>.
      </li>
      <li><code>--_min-height</code>: the height, set by the size classes.</li>
      <li>
        <code>--_motion</code>: the motion multiplier, <code>0</code> turns transitions
        off.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>--_padding-inline</code>: the inline padding, set by the size
        classes.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Other components use the same names where they mean the same thing, like
      <code>--_size</code> for the height of a Badge. Every component page lists its
      properties under Component properties, marked as inputs or outputs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="set-them-on-the-component">Set them on the component</h2>
    <p>
      Theme tokens can be set on any parent, since they're inherited. <code
        >--_</code
      > properties can't: the component declares its own value, and a declared value
      always beats an inherited one. So set them on the component itself.
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`/* Does nothing: every .ui-button sets its own --_icon-size */
.toolbar {
  --_icon-size: 1.25em;
}

/* Works */
.toolbar .ui-button {
  --_icon-size: 1.25em;
}

/* Theme tokens are inherited, so a parent is fine */
.toolbar {
  --ui-button-border-radius: var(--radius-round);
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <p>
      An inline <code>style</code> works too, for a one-off:
    </p>
    <Example centered>
      <button
        class="ui-button ui-filled"
        style="--_accent: var(--ui-green)"
        type="button"
      >
        Green filled
      </button>
      <button
        class="ui-button ui-outlined"
        style="--_accent: var(--ui-green)"
        type="button"
      >
        Green outlined
      </button>
    </Example>
    <p>
      Prefer the inputs, like <code>--_accent</code>, over the outputs, like <code
        >--_bg-color</code
      >. Hover, active and disabled states set the outputs too, so overriding <code
        >--_bg-color</code
      > freezes the background in every state, while a new <code>--_accent</code
      > flows through all of them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="unlayered-or-your-own-layer">Unlayered or your own layer</h2>
    <p>
      All OPUI styles live in <DocLink href="/guide/concepts#cascade-layers"
        >cascade layers</DocLink
      >. Where you put your overrides decides how they compete with the library,
      and with each other.
    </p>
    <h3 id="unlayered">Unlayered</h3>
    <p>
      CSS outside any layer beats every layer, whatever the specificity. Simple,
      and fine for most apps:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`.ui-button.compact {
  --_min-height: var(--size-7);
  --_padding-inline: 1ex;
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <h3 id="your-own-layer">Your own layer</h3>
    <p>
      If you use layers yourself, add one after <code>utils</code>. It beats the
      library and still loses to your unlayered CSS, so one-off fixes keep
      working:
    </p>
    <Code
      code={`@layer openprops, theme, normalize, components.prose, components.root, components.extended, utils, overrides;
@layer theme.palette, theme.base, theme.custom;

@layer overrides {
  .ui-badge {
    --_size: var(--size-5);
  }
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <p>
      Layers are ordered by where they're first mentioned, so a layer you first
      use after the OPUI imports already lands after <code>utils</code>.
      Spelling out the full order is just nicer to read.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="more-examples">More examples</h2>
    <p>Bigger icons in a toolbar, without touching the buttons elsewhere:</p>
    <Code
      code={`.toolbar .ui-button {
  --_icon-size: var(--size-5);
}`}
      lang="css"
    />
    <p>A critical button that skips its color transitions:</p>
    <Code
      code={`<button class="ui-button ui-filled ui-critical" style="--_motion: 0" type="button">
  Delete
</button>`}
      lang="html"
    />
    <p>
      Need to change it everywhere instead? Then it's a theme token, see <DocLink
        href="/guide/theme-tokens">Theme tokens</DocLink
      > or try the <DocLink href="/guide/theme-generator"
        >Theme generator</DocLink
      >. <DocLink href="/guide/theming">Theming</DocLink> covers the palette, density,
      spacing, corners, fonts, elevation, motion and contrast.
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
